+`validate` sits outside that rule on purpose: it is a client of a running deployment, not a part of one, and depends on no `wyrd-*` crate (proposal 0017 §9). Its command-line surface is ten required flags — `--endpoint`, `--region`, `--bucket`, `--scenario`, `--duration`, `--workers`, `--seed`, `--out`, `--run-id`, `--driver-placement` — with S3 credentials taken from `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, else `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY`/`WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, else refused; half a pair, or a variable whose value is not UTF-8, is refused by name rather than skipped over to the next pair (an empty variable counts as unset). Parsing is strict, unlike `wyrd`'s: an unknown flag, a repeated flag, an empty value, a stray positional, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused by name rather than absorbed. Today the binary echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request. Its S3 client layer (`validate::client`, #741) is a stock `aws-sdk-s3` client — the one `wyrd-*`-free way to sign SigV4, since the gateway's own signer is a Wyrd crate — pointed at `--endpoint` with path-style addressing, the `--region`, and the resolved static credentials, over plain HTTP only (no TLS client until the rustls provider decision). SDK retries and stalled-stream protection are off, so a failure is reported, not retried away; instead every call is held to explicit connect, operation and body-idle deadlines. Failures come back as a typed `S3Error` that keeps apart an S3 error response (HTTP status, `<Code>` — or "no code" / "no body" — `<Message>`, `x-amz-request-id`), an unreadable response (a success the SDK cannot read, an error body that is not an S3 `<Error>` document, an error body cut short), no response, a timeout naming its phase, a request the SDK would not build, and an object body that failed or cannot be trusted. Only what arrived on the wire is reported: the request id is read from the response header, and a code the SDK fills in for an empty 404 is not passed off as the server's. Object bodies stream both ways: a PUT is fed from a caller's stream of exact declared length (a source that ends short, runs long or fails is refused, never stored as a different object), a GET is read piece by piece against its declared `Content-Length` (a GET response that declares none is refused, since a body cut short could not be told from a whole one), and neither is held whole. `aws-sdk-s3` is therefore a normal dependency of a shipped binary; the validator's tests serve a real Wyrd S3 gateway in-process from dev-dependencies, which the blackbox guard permits.
